    "question_text": "The reagent(s) used for softening the temporary hardness of water is(are)",
    "question_image": null,
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "6TVLe",
        "text": "\n$\\mathrm{Ca}_3\\left(\\mathrm{PO}_4\\right)_2$\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "TDbB5",
        "text": "\n$\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{OH})_2$\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "CY9Al",
        "text": "\n$\\mathrm{Na}_2 \\mathrm{CO}_3$\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "s9Ot6",
        "text": "\n$\\mathrm{NaOCl}$",
        "image": null
      }
    ],
    "correct_answer": "s9Ot6",
    "solution_text": "Softening the temporary hardness of water means conversion of bicarbonate into calcium carbonate (precipitation)\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\mathrm{Ca}\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_3\\right)_2+\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{OH})_2 \\longrightarrow \\\\\n2 \\mathrm{CaCO}_3 \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O} \\\\\n\\mathrm{Ca}\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_3\\right)_2+\\mathrm{Na}_2 \\mathrm{CO}_3 \\longrightarrow \\\\\n\\mathrm{CaCO}_3 \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{NaHCO}_3\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\nEven $\\mathrm{NaOCl}$ can be used, as in aqueous medium $\\overline{\\mathrm{O}} \\mathrm{Cl}$ gets hydrolysed and makes solution alkaline enough to cause precipitation of $\\mathrm{CaCO}_3$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{NaOCl}+\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O} & \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HOCl}+\\mathrm{NaOH} \\\\\n\\mathrm{OH}^{-}+\\mathrm{HCO}_3^{-} & \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_3^{2-}+\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nRepresentative elements (Hydrogen and its compounds)\nConceptual\nII",
    "solution_image": null,
    "topic": "Preparation and Properties of Water"
  }
